{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And he shall lay, etc.</b> And it does not say ״וְסָמְכוּ״ — that Aaron should lay his hands by himself and afterwards his sons would lay their hands. Now, there came into my hand a baraita in chapter ten of Menaḥot, and this is its language: Five who brought one sacrifice — all of them perform semikhah upon it; not all of them at once, but rather each one performs semikhah and withdraws — end quote. And Rambam ruled thus in chapter three of Hilkhot Ma'aseh HaKorbanot. According to this, the verse says <b>וְסָמַךְ</b> to say that they should perform semikhah one by one and not together. But one needs to know why it said it twice and was not satisfied with one, as written in the account in parashat Tzav (Leviticus 8:18). And perhaps it is because from a single instance I would say that since the sin on account of which the sacrifice comes depends on him — for he made the calf and not his sons — therefore Scripture specified by giving him precedence to allude to this matter, and one does not learn from it for every sacrifice; therefore it repeated and said twice <b>וְסָמַךְ</b> in the singular. From now on there is a correct reason why in parashat Tzav, in the account of the event, it did not use the singular except for the first one — for it suffices for the inference that we need of it, since it was not repeated in the commandment except to remove the error. And understand:"], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {0 "The two occurrences of וְסָמַךְ in singular are in Exodus 29:10 (the command) and Leviticus 8:14 (the fulfillment); the plural וְסָמְכוּ appears at Leviticus 8:18 in the account of a different offering."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:34:29.381449941Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}